{
  // X: Beyond the Frontier's sector map. games/xtension has its own.
  //
  // name, xbtfTextId, x and y are the game's own: the text tables name every sector, and the text
  // id encodes its cell (2YX in XBTF; x runs east, y south, from 1).
  //
  // gates are the game's own too: the compiled script, l/001.obj, builds the universe from the
  // map editor's data (see ScriptUniverse; openxt-import universe prints it), and every gate here
  // matches it.
  //
  // race: the script names the owner of every sector with a trading station, and each such
  // race here matches it. The rest have no owner in the script; their race is read from
  // elite-games.ru's map, xbtf.png, by sampling each cell's colour. The gates were first
  // read from the same map, at the midpoint of every shared edge, where a wall is black background
  // and a gate is not. Gates are symmetric; each lists the directions it opens to.
  //
  // stations are the game's own too, from the same editor data: each station where the script
  // places it, in metres from the sector's centre (x east, y up, z north), converted with the
  // factor the game's HUD uses for distances. Regenerate them with
  //     dotnet run --project tools/OpenXt.AssetImport -- universe --game xbtf \
  //         --update games/xbtf/data/sectors/sectors.json
  // which rewrites only the station lists. Two stations the script places have no station record of
  // their own, so the roster entry the elite-games.ru map shows in their place stands in: the
  // Pirate Anarchy Port is pirate_base, the Unknown Xenon Ship in XBTF's Xenon sector 7-6 is
  // xenon_station.
  //
  // They agree with the site's Excel map (mapxbtf.rar), the earlier source, in 45 of the 51
  // sectors it lists stations for; apart from the two stand-ins, the one difference is Company
  // Pride's silicon mine, which the sheet draws as an ore mine.
  //
  // A mod changes the map by layering its own data/sectors/sectors.json; entries merge by id.
